WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
A brushless drive usually splits its loop between firmware and a separate

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.180
gate driver.

3
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:11.640
SKU-1 puts both on one die and keeps the loop in hardware.

4
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.614
Commands arrive from the host, and the processor sets the loop up.

5
00:00:15.614 --> 00:00:20.754
But it never sits inside the loop, so firmware timing never decides

6
00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:22.454
when a gate switches.

7
00:00:22.454 --> 00:00:26.539
The torque command turns into three phase drive in fixed hardware,

8
00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:31.619
so the loop's latency is known, with a target under 1 microsecond.

9
00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:36.670
Seven PWM channels switch three half bridges, on a rail anywhere from

10
00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.990
5 to 120 volts.

11
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.070
That voltage is why the die is BCD.

12
00:00:43.114 --> 00:00:46.914
Where the rotor is, and how much current flows in each phase,

13
00:00:46.914 --> 00:00:49.874
both return straight to the regulator.

14
00:00:50.354 --> 00:00:52.454
Firmware is never in that path.

15
00:00:52.457 --> 00:00:56.597
On a thermal, overcurrent or overvoltage fault, the gates shut.

16
00:00:56.597 --> 00:01:01.397
And they stay shut, latched, until someone decides otherwise.

17
00:01:01.397 --> 00:01:03.054
SKU-1 is pre-silicon.

18
00:01:03.054 --> 00:01:05.414
Every figure is a target.

19
00:01:05.414 --> 00:01:08.274
The converter's isolation from switching edges,

20
00:01:08.874 --> 00:01:12.374
and the loop deadline under bus load, must still be proven

21
00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:15.494
Bring us your motor and your bridge.

22
00:01:15.494 --> 00:01:19.054
We will tell you what SKU-1 must prove.
